ightly by the absence of any local polling.", "watch": "Any late-breaking polling or a surge of Democratic outside spending in the Green Bay media market would be the first sign the race is competitive.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tony Wied (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Tony Wied seeks a second term in Wisconsin's 8th District, a seat with an R+8 partisan lean, against Democrat Rick Crosson, a first-time candidate who won a three-way primary. Wied ran unopposed for renomination and has emphasized tariffs, the farm bill, and committee work in local coverage.\n\nThe race has drawn little competitive attention: no public general-election polls are available, every major race rater places it at Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Crosson roughly a one-in-eight chance. Wied's financial edge is decisive, with more than $1 million raised against Crosson's $70,000. The district's fundamentals and the incumbent's resources make Crosson's path dependent on a broad national shift well beyond what current evidence suggests.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0582, "margin": -10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.052, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0699, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.033, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0472, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0442, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0408, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -13.0}]}